WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.098 --> 00:00:09.220
In 1867 a young man named John Muir walked a thousand miles across the United States.

2
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:14.842
Muir had already had many outdoor adventures in his life, but this journey was special because it almost

3
00:00:14.882 --> 00:00:15.483
didn't happen.

4
00:00:16.143 --> 00:00:20.565
Six months earlier, a factory accident had temporarily blinded Muir, changing him forever.

5
00:00:21.105 --> 00:00:25.447
The despair he felt at the thought of never again seeing the wilderness was so strong that he

6
00:00:25.507 --> 00:00:27.708
vowed to spend the rest of his life studying nature.

7
00:00:28.288 --> 00:00:33.871
He later also decided to share his passion by starting a career as a writer—in order to "entice

8
00:00:33.911 --> 00:00:35.472
people to look at nature's loveliness."

9
00:00:36.192 --> 00:00:42.315
Over the years John Muir's explorations and writings changed people's views of wilderness and inspired many to take

10
00:00:42.355 --> 00:00:43.755
up the cause of preservation.

11
00:00:44.396 --> 00:00:48.778
John Muir was born on April 21, 1838, in Dunbar, Scotland.

12
00:00:49.518 --> 00:00:52.980
One of eight siblings, he spent hours exploring the outdoors as a young boy.

13
00:00:53.736 --> 00:00:58.218
Muir attended school until he was 11, when his family moved to America and settled on a farm

14
00:00:58.258 --> 00:00:58.978
in Wisconsin.

15
00:00:59.598 --> 00:01:04.340
Muir's strict, religious father put his children to work in the fields from dawn to dusk; there was

16
00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:05.341
no time for school.

17
00:01:05.901 --> 00:01:09.783
But Muir was curious about the world around him and began teaching himself with books.

18
00:01:10.463 --> 00:01:15.125
By the age of 15, he had developed a keen interest in mechanical devices and hoped to become

19
00:01:15.165 --> 00:01:15.725
an inventor.

20
00:01:16.325 --> 00:01:21.047
He received permission from his father to rise five hours before dawn each morning so he could read

21
00:01:21.127 --> 00:01:22.588
books and tinker with machinery.

22
00:01:23.188 --> 00:01:27.690
To make sure sleep didn't steal this precious time, he invented an alarm clock to tip his bed

23
00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:28.971
and knock him onto the floor.

24
00:01:29.651 --> 00:01:34.733
Then he would hurry down to the cellar where, working by candlelight, he crafted clocks and other gadgets.

25
00:01:35.493 --> 00:01:39.435
When he was 22 years old, Muir displayed his inventions at the state fair.

26
00:01:40.095 --> 00:01:44.037
His work was praised for its ingenuity, which encouraged Muir to continue his education.

27
00:01:44.657 --> 00:01:47.159
He decided to study at the University of Wisconsin.

28
00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:50.100
Muir pursued his interest in invention in college.

29
00:01:50.768 --> 00:01:54.849
In fact, he kept up with his coursework by building a desk that could open up a book

30
00:01:54.889 --> 00:01:57.770
for a set time and then automatically replace it with another.

31
00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.311
But Muir's interest in the natural world was growing.

32
00:02:01.451 --> 00:02:07.773
In 1864 he left college to visit Canada, where he wandered in the wilderness, collecting botanical specimens and

33
00:02:07.873 --> 00:02:08.914
sketching in a notebook.

34
00:02:09.514 --> 00:02:16.336
To support himself, Muir worked at a Canadian sawmill and broom-making factory, where his mechanical skills proved invaluable.

35
00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:22.904
Muir left Canada in 1866, returning to the United States to accept a job at an Indiana factory

36
00:02:22.944 --> 00:02:24.285
and to study the local landscape.

37
00:02:25.026 --> 00:02:31.931
Disaster struck on March 6, 1867; a tool slipped from Muir's hand and pierced his right eye, causing

38
00:02:31.971 --> 00:02:33.472
him to go blind in both eyes.

39
00:02:34.112 --> 00:02:38.215
The threat of permanent blindness allowed Muir to see what was truly important to him.

40
00:02:38.855 --> 00:02:44.439
When Muir's vision returned, he dedicated himself to the study of nature, writing, "I bade adieu to mechanical

41
00:02:44.479 --> 00:02:48.442
inventions, determined to devote the rest of my life to the inventions of God."

42
00:02:49.122 --> 00:02:55.486
As soon as his eyesight returned, Muir began his thousand-mile walk, traveling from Kentucky to Florida and observing

43
00:02:55.586 --> 00:02:56.746
plant life along the way.

44
00:02:57.387 --> 00:03:00.268
He then took a boat to Cuba, where he spent a month in the jungle.

45
00:03:00.848 --> 00:03:05.211
Muir hoped to continue his trip with a raft ride down the Amazon River, but he could not

46
00:03:05.291 --> 00:03:07.052
find a boat sailing to South America.

47
00:03:07.732 --> 00:03:11.954
Seeing cheap fares to California, he decided to visit San Francisco instead.

48
00:03:12.535 --> 00:03:14.496
This decision would change the rest of his life.

49
00:03:15.252 --> 00:03:20.995
Within days of his arrival, he started walking to Yosemite Valley, in the Sierra Nevada, a vast mountain

50
00:03:21.035 --> 00:03:22.696
range in California and Nevada.

51
00:03:23.317 --> 00:03:29.620
There a rancher hired Muir to herd sheep, offering Muir an extraordinary opportunity to study Yosemite and the

52
00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:30.420
mountains around it.

53
00:03:31.061 --> 00:03:35.463
He was awed by the beauty and serenity of Yosemite and the Sierra Nevada, a place he called

54
00:03:35.743 --> 00:03:36.784
the "Range of Light."

55
00:03:37.364 --> 00:03:41.667
Muir worked a variety of jobs but spent as much time as he could studying the mountain range.

56
00:03:42.327 --> 00:03:46.625
As word of Muir's knowledge spread, visitors began to seek him out for guided tours.

57
00:03:47.225 --> 00:03:51.526
By 1871 Muir had begun to write about the natural wonders of the landscape.

58
00:03:52.127 --> 00:03:54.747
He also shared his views on the damage caused by people.

59
00:03:55.348 --> 00:03:57.088
Tourists left garbage scattered about.

60
00:03:57.668 --> 00:04:03.030
Farmers' sheep devoured everything before them—for which he called them "hooved locusts."

61
00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:08.732
In 1873 Muir left the wilderness of the valley to enlist support for its preservation through his writing.

62
00:04:09.352 --> 00:04:15.296
His articles became popular among both the common people of California and influential figures across the country.

63
00:04:15.896 --> 00:04:21.080
Although Muir had many friends in California and many more with whom he exchanged letters, his long walks

64
00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:23.181
were usually done alone, and he was lonely.

65
00:04:23.782 --> 00:04:29.646
In 1880 Muir finally found love and married, settling down in California to run his father-in-law's ranch.

66
00:04:30.266 --> 00:04:35.830
He spent the winters on the ranch—"lost and choked in agricultural needs," as he wrote—but he continued to

67
00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:38.992
travel during the rest of the year and write about his adventures in the wild.

68
00:04:39.584 --> 00:04:43.846
He especially loved to tell the story of a trek across an Alaskan glacier on which he was

69
00:04:43.886 --> 00:04:45.587
accompanied by a dog named Stickeen.

70
00:04:46.187 --> 00:04:52.029
Together Muir and the brave dog crossed a treacherous sliver of ice—a long, narrow bridge hundreds of feet

71
00:04:52.089 --> 00:04:52.549
in the air.

72
00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:58.812
Throughout the next decade, Muir continued to call for preservation of the Yosemite area, saying that wilderness is

73
00:04:58.872 --> 00:05:01.633
necessary for people's physical and spiritual health.

74
00:05:02.193 --> 00:05:07.876
He encouraged city dwellers to experience the wonders of nature, writing, "Come to the woods, for here is

75
00:05:07.976 --> 00:05:15.366
rest." The United States Congress finally declared the Yosemite region a national park in 1890, but Muir pressed

76
00:05:15.426 --> 00:05:17.966
for further expansion of the federal national parks system.

77
00:05:18.587 --> 00:05:24.928
In 1903, when President Theodore Roosevelt visited Yosemite Valley, he camped alone with Muir for several days.

78
00:05:25.589 --> 00:05:29.470
The two men became lifelong friends, and Muir gained a powerful ally.

79
00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:36.451
During his presidency, Roosevelt established five national parks and protected other sites, such as the Grand Canyon, by

80
00:05:36.491 --> 00:05:38.232
declaring them national monuments.

81
00:05:38.892 --> 00:05:43.657
The impact Muir had on the president is one reason he is now considered the father of America's

82
00:05:43.697 --> 00:05:44.437
national parks.

83
00:05:45.278 --> 00:05:50.763
Muir continued to travel and advocate for preservation until his seventies, and he never regretted his choice to

84
00:05:50.823 --> 00:05:54.226
abandon a career as an inventor to follow his love for the outdoors.

85
00:05:54.867 --> 00:05:57.469
He died on December 24, 1914.

86
00:05:58.010 --> 00:06:03.675
During his lifetime he had over three hundred articles and ten books published, and his influence extends to

87
00:06:03.695 --> 00:06:04.336
the modern day.

88
00:06:04.916 --> 00:06:10.779
He was a founder of the Sierra Club, an organization still committed to helping people "explore, enjoy, and

89
00:06:10.819 --> 00:06:11.739
protect the planet."

90
00:06:12.379 --> 00:06:17.681
Many people are reminded of his contributions as they walk in the Muir Woods, a grove of thousand-year-old

91
00:06:17.742 --> 00:06:18.822
trees in California.

92
00:06:19.442 --> 00:06:25.545
This grove might have been destroyed if Muir had not helped people recognize "that wildness is necessity; that

93
00:06:25.665 --> 00:06:28.826
mountain parks and reservations are fountains of life."

94
00:06:29.567 --> 00:06:35.177
John Muir inspired people everywhere to embrace the wonders of nature, and his legacy survives in today's efforts

95
00:06:35.237 --> 00:06:37.523
to preserve wilderness around the world.
